      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. one more thing, why does the body react so differently to these? like what's actually happening with the insulin during those spikes vs the slow burn? i want to be able to explain the \"why\" to my client.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The difference comes down to how quickly the carbohydrates are broken down into glucose in the digestive tract. High GI foods are like a sudden rush of sugar, causing the body to dump a large amount of insulin quickly to manage the sudden glucose surge. Low GI foods, however, release glucose slowly, meaning the pancreas only needs to release a steady, manageable trickle of insulin. This slower, more consistent action prevents the extreme stress on the body's insulin system.",
      "source": "target",
